course: books
lesson: 94
title: Just So Stories, The Cat that Walked by Himself, part 2: the bargain, the Bat and the Baby
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Wild Cow comes next and promises her milk every day for the wonderful grass; she becomes the Giver of Good Food. Then no one else comes, so the Cat walks up by himself and asks to come in. The Woman says they need no more friends or servants. «I am not a friend, and I am not a servant,» says the Cat. «I am the Cat who walks by himself, and I wish to come into your cave.»
explain: The Cat flatters her: she is wise and beautiful and should not be cruel. The Woman makes a bargain: one word in his praise and he may come into the Cave; two words and he may sit by the fire; three and he may drink the warm white milk three times a day. «I never shall,» she says. The Cat asks the curtain, the fire and the milk-pots to remember.
explain: The Cat hides in the woods for a long time, till the Woman forgets him. Only the little upside-down Bat knows where he is and brings him news: there is a new Baby in the Cave, fond of soft, tickly, warm things and of being played with. «Then my time has come,» says the Cat.
explain: One busy morning the Baby cries outside the Cave. The Cat pats his cheek and tickles him with his tail, and the Baby laughs. The Woman, not seeing who it is, says «A blessing on that Wild Thing», and whoosh, the horse-skin curtain falls: the Cat is inside. Then she lets him play a string game with the spindle-whorl until the Baby laughs and falls asleep, and the Cat purrs him a sleep-song. «That was wonderfully done,» says the Woman; puff, the smoke comes down, and the Cat is sitting by the fire.
fact: Wild Cow comes «holding her wild head high that her wild horns should not catch in the wild trees», and everything happens «just the same as before».
fact: Her new name: «the Giver of Good Food. She will give us the warm white milk for always and always and always.»
fact: The Woman to the Cat: «I have braided up my hair, and I have put away the magic blade-bone, and we have no more need of either friends or servants in our Cave.»
fact: The Cat «pretended to be sorry» and said «You are very wise and very beautiful. You should not be cruel even to a Cat.» The Woman: «I knew I was wise, but I did not know I was beautiful.»
fact: The bargain: one word of praise, into the Cave; two words, by the fire; three words, the warm white milk three times a day «for always and always and always».
fact: The Woman does not tell the Man, the Horse and the Dog about the bargain, «because she was afraid that they might not like it».
fact: The Bat says: «There is a Baby in the Cave. He is new and pink and fat and small, and the Woman is very fond of him.» The Cat asks: «but what is the Baby fond of?»
fact: That busy morning the Woman carried the crying Baby outside and gave him a handful of pebbles to play with, but he went on crying.
fact: When the Cat plays with the Baby, the Bat says «a Wild Thing from the Wild Woods is most beautifully playing with your Baby», and the Woman answers «A blessing on that Wild Thing whoever he may be, for I was a busy woman this morning and he has done me a service.»
fact: The curtain falls «whoosh!» because «it remembered the bargain»; the Cat says «for you have spoken a word in my praise, and now I can sit within the Cave for always». The Woman is very angry; she shuts her lips tight, takes up her spinning and says nothing.
fact: The Cat's game: the Woman ties thread to her «little clay spindle whorl» and drags it; the Cat pats it, rolls head over heels and pounces until the Baby laughs; then the Cat purrs «loud and low, low and loud».
fact: The second word: «That was wonderfully done. No question but you are very clever, O Cat.» The smoke comes down «puff!» and the Cat is by the fire.
fact: Real life: a spindle whorl is a small weight of clay or stone on a stick, used for spinning thread for thousands of years; the spinning-wheel, which Kipling also mentions, was invented much later, about a thousand years ago.
fact: Real life: most grown-up cats cannot digest cow's milk well, and it can upset their tummies; water is the best drink for a cat.
fact: Real life: bats really do hang upside down and find their way in the dark by listening to the echoes of their own squeaks; cats really purr, and kittens play-hunt string to practise catching prey.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «rizz» = charm, the power to win people over; «aye» (Scotland, North England) = yes; «proper job» (Cornwall) = well done; «just now» (South Africa) = in a while, later.
exercise: What does Wild Cow promise the Woman? | Her milk every day | To carry the family on her back from place to place for always | To guard the Cave at night | To catch the mice in the Cave
exercise: What is Wild Cow's new name? | The Giver of Good Food | The First Friend | The First Servant | The Warm White One
exercise: Why does the Cat go up to the Cave by himself? | No other Wild Thing moves in the woods | The Dog invited him to come and see the fire and taste the roast mutton | He was hungry | The Bat told him to
exercise: What does the Woman say when the Cat asks about Wild Cow? | Go back, we need no more friends or servants | Come in and have some warm white milk with the Baby | Wild Cow ran away | Ask the Dog
exercise: What does the Cat say he is? | Neither a friend nor a servant | A friend and a servant at once, the very best of both | A servant only | A Dog
exercise: How does the Cat try to soften the Woman? | He calls her very wise and very beautiful | He brings her a dead mouse wrapped in a leaf as a present | He cries | He sings
exercise: What does the Woman say back? | She knew she was wise, not beautiful | That she is not wise at all, only a busy woman with a Baby and a Cave to keep | That cats should never speak | That she will call the Dog
exercise: One word of praise lets the Cat do what? | Come into the Cave | Drink the warm white milk three times a day for always | Sit by the warm fire inside | Sleep on the Baby's bed
exercise: Two words? | Sit by the fire | Come into the Cave | Drink the warm milk | Go hunting with the Man and the Dog
exercise: Three words? | Drink the warm white milk three times a day | Be called the First Friend and sit in the Man's own place by the fire | Sit by the fire | Go away
exercise: What does the Woman say each time the Cat asks for more? | «I never shall» | «Perhaps tomorrow» | «Go and ask the Man» | «Yes, of course, if you behave»
exercise: What does the Cat ask to remember the bargain? | The Curtain, the Fire and the Milk-pots | The Dog, the Horse and the Cow, who were standing by | The Moon | The Bat
exercise: Why doesn't the Woman tell the Man about the bargain? | She is afraid they might not like it | She forgets, because she is very busy with the cooking and the Baby | The Cat asks her not to | The Man is asleep
exercise: Who knows where the Cat is hiding? | Only the little upside-down Bat | The Dog | The Woman | Nobody at all
exercise: What news does the Bat bring one evening? | There is a new Baby in the Cave | The Cow has run back to the Wet Wild Woods with her calf | The Dog is ill | The fire went out
exercise: What is the Baby fond of, the Bat says? | Soft, tickly, warm things | Loud noises, like the Horse stamping and the Dog barking at night | Milk only | Pebbles
exercise: What does the Woman give the crying Baby first? | A handful of pebbles | A spindle-whorl on a long thread, to pull along the floor | Milk | A bone
exercise: What does the Cat do to the Baby? | Pats his cheek and tickles him with his tail | Sings him a song from the Wet Wild Woods, very loudly | Licks him | Runs away
exercise: What does the Woman say when she hears the Baby laugh? | «A blessing on that Wild Thing» | «Who is there? Go away at once, Wild Thing!» | «Bad Cat!» | «Thank you, Dog»
exercise: What happens to the horse-skin curtain? | It falls down, whoosh | It catches fire | It flies away | Nothing
exercise: What does the Woman do after the Cat gets in? | Shuts her lips tight and spins, very angry | Laughs and gives him a bowl of milk and a place by the fire | Calls the Dog | Cries
exercise: What does the Cat play with to make the Baby laugh? | Thread tied to the spindle whorl | The Baby's own toes, which he pats one by one until they wriggle | A ball of wool | A mouse
exercise: What «song» does the Cat sing to keep the Baby asleep? | He purrs | He meows a lullaby | He hums softly | He whistles
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, what did the Woman give Wild Horse to eat? | Wonderful dried grass | Roast bones | Milk | Fish
test: Isla from Edinburgh: «aye, the Woman praised the Cat on purpose, she liked him.» (aye = yes) Put it right. | She praised him without knowing it was him | Right, she always loved cats and wanted him by the fire from the very first night | She never praised him | The Bat praised him
test: Why does the Cat ask what the Baby is fond of, not what the Woman is fond of? | To win the Woman through the Baby | He loves babies more than anything else in the whole Wet Wild Woods | He is hungry | He wants to be a baby
test: «A blessing on that Wild Thing whoever he may be.» Why does this count as praise? | A blessing is a word in his praise | She is shouting at him to go away, but the curtain does not understand that | It is a spell | She is talking to the Bat
test: What would happen if the Woman had seen it was the Cat? | She might not have blessed him | The curtain would fall anyway, because curtains fall whenever a baby laughs | The Dog would come | Nothing
test: Which is TRUE in this part? | The Woman keeps the bargain secret | The Bat is the Cat's enemy and tells the Woman where the Cat is hiding | The Cow refuses to give milk | The Cat cries for real
test: Real life: is cow's milk good for grown-up cats? | Not really; water is best | Yes, it is all a cat needs to stay healthy, and it should have lots of it every day | Only on Sundays | Only warm milk
test: Real life: how do bats find their way in the dark? | By echoes of their squeaks | By glowing in the dark like fireflies, so they can see the branches | By smell only | They don't fly at night
test: Real life: which came much later than the spindle whorl? | The spinning-wheel, about a thousand years ago | Thread | Clay | Sheep
test: Real life: why do kittens chase string? | It is play-hunting practice | They hate string and want to tear every piece of it to bits | They are hungry for string | Someone taught them
test: Poppy: «just now the Cat'll walk in, the curtain's still up.» (just now = in a while, South Africa) When does the curtain fall? | When the Woman speaks the first word in his praise | At midnight | When the Dog barks | Never
test: Looking back: in part 1, what was the Dog's bargain? | Hunt and guard, for bones | Give milk three times a day, for the wonderful grass of the water-meadows | Carry them | Catch mice
test: Looking back: in part 1, what did the Cat say each time after the Dog and Horse made bargains? | That each was very foolish | That each was very wise | Nothing | That he would join them
test: Looking back: Pau Amma the Crab also refused to be obedient at the start. What did he say? | He would never obey the son of Adam | He would obey only the little girl-daughter, because she had golden scissors | He wanted to be a Cat | He would play with the Moon
test: Looking back: in the Taffy stories, who was told off for slang words? | Taffy, for saying «awful» | Teshumai | The Head Chief | The Stranger-man
test: Jake from Cornwall: «proper job, Cat got by the fire with ONE word.» (proper job = well done) Put it right. | It took two words | Right, one word did it all, the curtain and the fire together at the same moment | It took three words | No words at all were needed
test: ★★★ The Cat has «a lot of rizz» (charm). Which moment shows his charm working MOST cleverly? | Getting a blessing without the Woman knowing it was him | Saying «Nenni!» | Hiding in the woods | Asking the Bat
test: ★★★ Careful: the Bat calls the Woman «my Hostess and Wife of my Host and Mother of my Host's Son». Who is the Host's Son? | The Baby | The Cat | The Dog | The Man
test: ★★★ After this part, how many of the three things in the bargain does the Cat have, and which is still to come? | Two; the milk is still to come | One; the fire and milk are to come | Three; all three things are done | None; nothing has happened so far
exam: Identify the terms of the bargain concluded between the Woman and the Cat. | One word of praise for the Cave, two for the fire, three for the milk | Three words for the Cave, two for the fire, one for the milk | The Cat to catch mice in return for milk | The Cat to guard the Baby for bones
exam: Ascertain the informant who conveyed to the Cat news of the Baby's birth. | The Bat | The Dog, the First Friend | The Horse | The Wild Cow
exam: It is given that the curtain fell of its own accord. Select the response most consistent with the narrative's explanation. | It remembered the bargain | It was dislodged by a strong wind from the Wet Wild Woods, the horse-skin being old | The Baby pulled it | The Man cut it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the suitability of cow's milk for adult cats? | Many adult cats digest it poorly, and water is the proper drink | It is their only food | It cures all illnesses | It is poisonous to every cat
